.he "du (1)                       8/16/26                       du (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
du
- summarize disk usage
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
du [ -s ] [ -a ] [ name ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Du gives the number of blocks in all files and, recursively, the
directories within each name.  With no name, the current directory is
used.
.sp
A file that has two links to it is counted only once.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-s	Give only the grand total of each name.

.ti -10
-a	Give an entry for each file as well as each directory.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The original counted kilobytes; here a block is 512 bytes, the way du
counted before Berkeley changed it, because the inode keeps a size and
not a block count.  Indirect blocks are not counted.
.sp
A special file counts as nothing - its device number lives where a
file keeps its size, and it is not a size.
.sp
A file named as an argument is not listed unless -a or -s is given.
.sp
The list of files already counted through a second link holds two
hundred entries; beyond that, a file with several links is counted
once per link.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1)
